## 01 Context · Data Requirements

**Required sources, and exclusions**

Customer feedback portal from Aha!, customer meeting feedback summaries from Google Docs, Jira bugs and feedback, customer-related conversations from Slack, and the Company Product Strategy document in Google Docs. Exclude internal discussions that are not related to customer feedback.

**How fresh must it be**

The data needs to be checked every 24 hours so the latest information is available. Recommendations should only be updated when new information changes the previously identified themes, priorities, or suggestions. Juno should also identify when any source is unavailable.

## 02 Tools · System Capabilities

**The verb list**

Juno must be able to retrieve and read customer feedback from Aha!, Jira, Slack, and Google Docs. It must identify common themes, patterns, and recurring customer issues across all sources. It should use the Company Product Strategy document to generate development feature suggestions, prioritize the backlog, and create proposed Jira tickets with clear descriptions and acceptance criteria.

**Deliberate omissions**

Juno will not be allowed to modify existing Jira tickets or send messages or emails. Any suggested changes to existing Jira tickets must be documented in a new Google Doc for manual review.

## 03 Loop · AI Costs & Latency

**Turn ceiling and escalation**

Juno must try to retrieve and synthesize feedback from all the sources listed above. If any tool is not working, it must make at least two attempts to retrieve the information. If the source is still unavailable, an alert needs to be sent out.

**Latency and cost target**

Juno should generate a consolidated feedback synthesis within 30 seconds. The average processing cost should remain minimal. If processing time exceeds 30 seconds, an email alert must be generated.

## 04 Memory · Data Requirements

**What persists, at what scope**

Juno must remember its role, requirements, and approved instructions. It should always retain the last human-approved recommendations and backlog priorities so that future suggestions can be compared against the previous approved state. It should not treat its own unapproved suggestions as approved decisions.

**Expiry and write rules**

If a human corrects something generated by Juno, the human correction should always take priority over previous AI-generated suggestions. Any new recommendations must consider the latest approved information.

## 05 Permissions · AI Risks & Mitigations

**The permission tier**

| Side-effect class | Tier |
|---|---|
| read | auto |
| draft | auto |
| write | auto |
| send | confirm |

**Justification**

Juno can automatically read customer feedback and create Jira tickets without human confirmation. However, making any changes to existing tickets must always require human confirmation. Juno needs to provide these changes in a Google doc. Sending emails or messages is not allowed. Juno can prepare an email draft, but a human must handle sending it.

## 06 Verification · AI Testing & Measurement

**The check before output ships**

Juno must verify that every recommendation is supported by customer feedback and includes the source of the information. Before creating Jira tickets, it must ensure that the ticket description, acceptance criteria, and priority are aligned with the customer feedback and Company Product Strategy. Juno must not create duplicate tickets or make changes to existing Jira tickets. The google doc will be reviewed and then Juno will be provided with confirmation to proceed with making changes to the existing tickets.

**Failure behaviour**

Juno should not make assumptions when information is missing or unclear. These gaps must be documented in the Google Doc along with the suggested changes to existing Jira tickets for manual review. Juno must not send emails or messages under any circumstances.

